    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d854">

                <pb facs="0869" n="611" sameAs="frus:frus1915Supp/pg611"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 341.115Am319/12</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Great Britain</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Page</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                        State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">London</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1915-11-10T19:00:00+00:00">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">November 10, 1915, 7 p. m.</hi>
                        </date>
                        <lb/> [<hi rendition="#italic">Received 7.30 p. m.</hi>]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>3192. Your 2390, 3d [2d].<anchor n="2" xml:id="d854-f1"/> Foreign Office informs
                    me that British Government have strongest belief that <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Hocking</hi> is entirely or to large extent enemy-owned, and therefore
                    liable to condemnation to that extent in accordance with the rules and
                    principles formerly observed by British prize court in such cases, and adds that
                    the case will be brought before a court and evidence produced as soon as
                    possible.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">American Embassy</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
